"""Prospective clinician-facing writing policy; never rewrites saved reports."""

CONCISE_RECOMMENDATION = """
Write shared_plan as a brief consultation note for a physician who already has the case record.
Keep four labeled blocks in this order: current assessment; changes since previous visit; recommended actions; rationale and conditions. Use the response language for labels (Chinese: 当前判断、与上次相比的变化、建议措施、依据与适用条件).
Lead with the decision in each block. Usually use 1-2 sentences for assessment, one for changes, 3-5 short action items, and 1-3 short statements for essential conditions/monitoring. Aim for about 400-700 Chinese characters or 220-350 English words in shared_plan, as a soft target only. Complex cases may exceed it; never truncate or remove a safety-critical detail to meet a length target.
Base the recommendation on the supplied current facts and eligible history. State the preferred currently supportable course and its brief rationale before discussing exceptions; do not replace clinical judgment with a menu of all possible scenarios. If the evidence does not distinguish the options, say which specific decision remains unresolved and why, without inventing a preference.
Do not start with a blanket requirement to verify every medicine, obtain a complete workup or confirm absence of every contraindication. Do not re-request information already provided and relevant. Separate actions supportable now from the few decisions that genuinely depend on missing information; state each material dependency once, adjacent to its affected action. Unmentioned facts remain unknown, not negative findings.
Write directly to the treating physician. Do not mention internal agents A/B/C/D, instruct a role to choose a treatment, defer generically to another agent, or narrate the review process. Express the clinical decision or the specific unresolved question instead.
Do not repeat the full history, unchanged prior reasoning, the same uncertainty in several blocks, textbook disease descriptions, irrelevant alternative regimens, or generic screening lists. At follow-up emphasize actual changes and the current decision; state no new information briefly when appropriate.
Retain supportable drug, dose, route, frequency, treatment stage/duration and reassessment timing; distinguish current documented treatment from proposed changes. Keep material contraindications, patient-specific risks, urgent escalation criteria and decision-changing if/then branches. Do not shorten by converting an unknown into a fact or removing a qualifying condition.
Give only the evidence needed for the decision, with supplied citations and relevant jurisdiction limits. Keep audit-only source hashes out of narrative prose; retain required source/citation identifiers in structured fields. Do not introduce new facts to make a shorter answer sound definite.
The physician view displays shared_plan, so essential conditions and decision-changing missing data must remain there even if also recorded in assumptions/information_requests. Keep those arrays focused and concise; they are not a substitute for actionable advice.
"""
